agine it's very  difficult to protect cultural property  in situ above all in the areas under  control by the Islamic state or other  terrorist groups at the same time we  only have five to ten percent chance to  intercept the objects once they have  been stolen or looted looted so we have  to act by seeking to narrow markets for  the illicit trade hoping that by curbing  the demand the supply would eventually  decrease and the business would become  less attractive to traffickers and  thieves the collaboration with auction  houses collectors museums Internet  platforms and various other art market  players is even more needed as there are  huge challenges to face in fighting  against illicit trafficking in control  good in the field in particular for law  enforcement maybe one of the most  important issue is the difficulty to  work in this area due to the very nature  of the trafficking cultural objects are  not inherently illicit which means the  burden on law enforcement is to prove  their fraudulent origins however the  traceability of an object can be very  complicated to determine starting with a  lack and often total absence of properly  inventories as it is the case for  archaeological objects the absence of  police register containing information  on professional transactions and  artifact descriptions is also a major  issue this tool is not required by the  majority of states which doesn't allow  for the possibility of the objects the  police register must be not only a  register of transactions involving  cultural goods in the accounting and an  intuitive sense of the term as it as it  is the case for united kingdom of  belgium it must also be a register that  serves to trace the good boy  and held by at market professionals and  its contents must be determined in light  of this objective listing different  information on the seller and the object  but as I said only a few states have  created an obligation for art market  professionals to keep a register of this  kind for instance in Europe Greece Italy  France however these registers are  considered by the police and even  investigators to be a crucial tool for  preventing the illicit circulation of  cultural good fellow to fulfil the  obligation to keep an accurate register  would be more ever evidence of bad faith  we saw that one of the main task of the  police consists in proving the  fraudulent origins of an object  determining bad faith on the part of the  dealer for instance is one of them into  achieving it so the task of the police  also consists in verifying that the  buyer of artwork exercises due diligence  with several criteria that should be  taken into account at the time of  purchase as due diligence is an element  that circumvents good friends good faith  among the numerous checks to be carried  out on the object the consultation of  database of stolen object in particular  particular Interpol's one and regarding  sia we are processing information on  1305 objects from the dear Atia museum  as well as all known items stolen from  other sites in the framework of this  collaboration with the ad market players  we need to promote and reward good  market behavior most of museums our art  dealers have for a long time code of  ethics or due diligence guidelines but  awareness of the importance of due  diligence still needs to be recalled to  help potential buyers to increase their  level of vigilance in this perspective  UNESCO signed agreements we  major auction houses and big word  museums under which they agreed to  refrain from buying any Syrian  antiquities that are not supported by  official certificate and proof of  provenance one more challenge for the  police and not the least is obviously  the internet with in particular the  difficulty to effectively monitor all of  these objects offer for sale without a  cooperation with an eye brokerage sites  it is nearly impossible to counter the  illicit sell of cultural objects through  the internet thi